==================================================================
DAY 47 - PART A: THEORY
Cloud IAM for Pods: EKS Pod Identity, IRSA, Workload Identity
Time: 40 minutes
==================================================================

1. THE PROBLEM
--------------
Your app must read an S3 bucket. Bad options:
  - put AWS access keys in a Secret: long-lived, leak-prone, hard to
    rotate, shared by everything that can read the Secret
  - give the NODE's IAM role S3 access: EVERY pod on every node gets it
Good option: give ONE ServiceAccount an IAM role. Only pods using that
ServiceAccount get short-lived credentials for that role.


2. EKS POD IDENTITY (newer, simplest)
-------------------------------------
  - add-on eks-pod-identity-agent (DaemonSet on every node)
  - "pod identity association": (cluster, namespace, serviceaccount)
    -> IAM role (trust policy: pods.eks.amazonaws.com)
  - the agent injects env vars; AWS SDKs fetch temporary credentials
    automatically


3. IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts, older, still common)
-------------------------------------------------------------
  - cluster has an OIDC provider; ServiceAccount tokens are OIDC tokens
  - IAM role trust policy trusts that OIDC provider + the exact
    "system:serviceaccount:<ns>:<name>" subject
  - ServiceAccount annotated:
      eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::<acct>:role/<role>
  - a webhook mounts a token; SDK calls sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity
(eksctl create iamserviceaccount, used yesterday for the LB
controller, sets up IRSA.)


4. AKS AND GKE
--------------
AKS Workload Identity: managed identity + federated credential; SA
  annotation azure.workload.identity/client-id, pod label
  azure.workload.identity/use: "true".
GKE Workload Identity Federation: grant IAM roles directly to
  principal://.../subject/ns/<ns>/sa/<sa>, or annotate the SA with
  iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account.
Same idea everywhere: SA -> federated -> cloud role, no keys.


5. SECRETS FROM CLOUD SECRET MANAGERS
-------------------------------------
External Secrets Operator: a SecretStore authenticates with workload
identity to AWS Secrets Manager / SSM, Azure Key Vault, GCP Secret
Manager; ExternalSecret objects sync values into Kubernetes Secrets
and refresh them. Secrets never live in Git.


6. LEAST PRIVILEGE
------------------
One role per app; scope to specific resources (one bucket ARN, not
"*"); separate roles per environment.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Never cloud keys in Secrets; never broad node roles.
- ServiceAccount -> IAM role via Pod Identity or IRSA.
- External Secrets syncs cloud secret stores into the cluster.
